A||||6||Tom88gt||87-88 V-6 trans fluid check???||||5||
Z||000000||Tom88gt||01-12-2000||09:54 AM||||What is the correct way to check the trans fluid level?  Warm, cold, does it matter?  The Haynes just says be able to touch trans with your hand (could be warm or cold).  Is there a noticable level difference?||||reg||5
Z||000001||ka4nkf||01-11-2000||09:12 PM||||Tom I prefer to check mine after it has been drivin about 20 miles at highway speed. It has expanded about all it is going to.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dave85||01-11-2000||09:36 PM||||The dipstick gives you the heads-up. It says check hot, with car in park at an idle. Oh yea, should be level.<P>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||01-12-2000||05:12 PM||||Drive the car for 20 minutes, put the transmission in each gear for three seconds (123NRRN321) in the order shown. Pull the dipstick out and wipe it off. Insert the dipstick all the way back down the tube and then pull it out and read the fluid level.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tom88gt||01-12-2000||05:27 PM||||OOPS!  I should have specified that it's a 5 speed.  The procedures y'all are describing sound like its for an auto.||||reg||11
Z||000005||fiero56||01-12-2000||05:47 PM||||IF your checking the fluid level for a manual, I don't really think it matters what the temp. The manual transmission fluid doesn't see nearly the temperature changes that the auto does. I've checked mine hot and cold, not really any difference.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tom88gt||01-13-2000||07:50 AM||||Thanks 56!||||reg||1
